use tools.py to talk to the unicorn, with an mpremote venv.

commands:

- upload: sync files with board
- run: sync, then run main.py
- dump: cat any crash.txt

see the file for more.

## simulator

`.venv/bin/python tools.py sim` draws the screens in a window on the Mac, so a
screen can be iterated on without uploading. it never touches the board.

- `--screen NAME`: start on draw_NAME. it can be any draw_* in screens.py, even
  one that isn't in SCREENS yet. default: the first in SCREENS
- `--fixture NAME`: show sim/fixtures/NAME.json. default: the most recently
  modified fixture. with none at all, it fetches one
- `--live`: fetch Info now (needs proj_secrets.py), save it as
  sim/fixtures/YYYYMMDD-HHMM.json and show it
- `--at HH:MM`: the time the clock starts at. default: the fixture's "at"
- `--png FILE --t SECONDS`: save one frame and exit, no window
- `--raw`: framebuffer colours as square pixels rather than LEDs

fixtures are `{"at": "HH:MM", "info": {...}}` and gitignored. copy one and
edit it for edge cases (no trains, frost, a long condition).

keys: space pause, `,` `.` step a frame while paused, r restart the screen (time_on_screen back to 0),
l LED/raw, left/right previous/next screen, q quit.

saving screens.py, icons.py, display.py or the fixture reloads it. if that
fails, the error is printed and the last good frame stays up.

the LED view's dim-pen lift (LED_LIFT in sim/simulator.py) is eyeballed, so
compare against the board before trusting subtle colours.

it shows one screen at a time where it rests after sliding in: no slide,
screen timer or status indicators, and switching screens just flicks.

what it can't catch: MicroPython language differences, memory and heap
fragmentation, speed, and anything in main.py, carousel.py, status.py or
lifecycle.py. the fakes do fail like the board on float coordinates,
non-string text and unknown fonts.
